From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jason chien <3936511+jason5545@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 07:20:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] kk: fragment shader interlock through Metal raster order
 groups

D3D12 rasterizer ordered views reach KosmicKrisp as
SPV_EXT_fragment_shader_interlock (vkd3d-proton). nir_to_msl printed
begin/end_invocation_interlock as "Unknown intrinsic", the MSL failed to
compile in the background, vkEndCommandBuffer returned
VK_ERROR_INVALID_SHADER_NV and the next vkQueueSubmit2 crashed.

Metal has no critical section; it orders accesses made through resources
declared [[raster_order_group(N)]]. With KosmicKrisp's bindless resources
the attribute only takes effect on a parameter, and on pointers derived
from that parameter: a texture handle cast from a plain address, or a
struct member reached that way, is not ordered (measured on M5 Max: N
overlapping fragments doing read+1+write, ordered only through a
parameter). So a fragment shader that uses an interlock gets:

- one parameter per storage texture type it loads,
  constant KkRogTexN *kk_rog_tN [[buffer(3 + N)]], where KkRogTexN holds
  the texture with [[raster_order_group(0)]], and every storage texture
  handle is loaded as ((constant KkRogTexN*)((constant uchar*)kk_rog_tN
  + (addr - (ulong)kk_rog_tN)))->t;
- device uchar *kk_rog_g [[buffer(11), raster_order_group(0)]], and the
  address of every device memory access is rebased onto it.

The command buffer's argument table grows to 12 bindings and points the
new ones at the sampler table: the parameters are only rebased onto,
never read. All storage accesses of such a shader are ordered, not just
those inside the interlock, which is stronger than required.

VK_EXT_fragment_shader_interlock is advertised with pixel and sample
interlock (raster order groups order per pixel, which covers samples).

Applies on Mesa ce576c29ed + fxgl/steamac v1.9.7 kosmickrisp patches
0001-0077.
